    "summary": "Translator’s commentary and the verse Y. XLIX, 12 describe a heroic settler who advances the holy system to the edge of sacred territory and gains cattle (Kine) for a seeking prophet. In the midst of struggles he anticipates a praised place called Garorfhian, while a community is prepared for worship and work because God has approached to aid His prophet and encourage his discouraged spirit. Vutaspa is depicted as intervening, addressing Ahura directly but Zarathustra indirectly, exhorting the prophet to continue propagating the teaching without being deterred by circumstances. With Zarathustra he would re-engage powerful helpers, yoking them like steeds to win God’s praise in Heaven by safely passing over every bridge of trial. Having heard Zarathustra’s metric words, he will use them in prayer, with hands stretched out in homage and vigour, seeking the object of his prayers through religious self-control and faithful action. His efforts are said to vie with the heavenly bodies in their praise of God; he perseveres as a ‘praiser-king’ and beseeches Ahura, the life-giver, to further the all-engrossing cause. The translated verse Y. XLIX, 12 asks Ahura Mazda what aids of grace He has for His invoking Zarathustra through His Righteous Order, and what aids of grace are given to the speaker through God’s Good Mind within his soul. The speaker declares he will continue to pray with praises, beseeching what is best according to God’s wished-for aim. Translator notes discuss the placement of the verse and an alternative grammatical rendering without adding new narrative content.",
